<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bedřich Smetana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Smetana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bedřich Smetana was a Czech composer, born in Litomyšl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, whose music became closely identified with his people's aspirations to a cultural and political revival; in his homeland he is regarded as the father of Czech music. After taking part briefly in the 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> uprising and working as a teacher and choirmaster in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>, he returned to Prague in the early 1860s to champion Czech opera, and The Bartered Bride (1866) achieved great popularity. Completely deaf by the end of 1874, he went on composing, notably the cycle of six symphonic poems Má vlast, which includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a>. A mental collapse early in 1884 led to his confinement in an asylum and his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana.jpg" alt="Bedřich Smetana" width="330" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smetana_LCCN2014716851_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tune belongs to a family that goes back to &quot;La Mantovana&quot;, an Italian song of about 1600, which circulated across Renaissance Europe under many names and which <b>Bedřich Smetana</b> used in &quot;Vltava&quot; from Má vlast.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li></ul></section>
